{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "187",
    "case_summary": "A teenaged male presented with a 7-year medical history of numerous asymptomatic reddish-brownish papules, nodules, and plaques over his whole body. The papules began from his bilateral eyelid margins and extended to his trunk and extremities. He also reported a 7-year medical history of polydipsia and polyuria. The patient had also been diagnosed with central diabetes insipidus, growth retardation, and hypothyroidism.\n\nPhysical examination revealed reddish-brown papules, nodules, and plaques on his periocular areas, perioral areas, neck, shoulder, axillary folds, cubital fossa, wrists, palms, umbilical region, lateral abdomen, inguinal area, anogenital area, popliteal fossa, and ankles.\n\n- Skin biopsy findings:    - Dense granulomatous infiltrate in the upper dermis consisting of histiocytes, lymphocytes, foam cells, multinucleated giant cells, eosinophils, and hemorrhage    - Immunohistochemical results: negative for CD1a and S-100, positive for CD68  - Images:    Image A Title: Reddish-brown papules and plaques on the neck    Image B Title: Reddish-brown papules and plaques on the umbilical region, lateral abdomen, inguinal area, and genital area    Image C Title: Hematoxylin-eosin stain (original magnification ×100)    Image D Title: Hematoxylin-eosin stain (original magnification ×200)",
